nted to see  another antibody on the same  slot in this case instead of the tail  it's a purple  purple is also an hrp activated  chromogen  which has very robust reliable  performance  and is my second choice might be the  same  level as teal they're my two most used  non-brown chromogens as i start to  create complex protocols  multiplexers is limited to bright fields  and homogeneous  and neither is using this amphit  technology to really boost your staining  intensity  we've had success with using it  in immunofluorescent assays as well  here's a great tumor example where we're  looking at  cd4 in green cd8 and red  and we  moving from two color multiplex to three  this is a great example of a project  that came  from a client interested in three  separate  specific antigens which they knew  were prominent in the type of tissue  however they were interested in what the  distribution  actually is are all three antigens  on the same cells do they have patches  within the tumor  and how about that really makes you  think of  how to construct this type of product  where you're able to look at each  antigen separately  and also any coexpression  so the colors we chose for this protocol  were teal purple and yellow that's  because they're very distinct colors  individually and helpfully  they allow for a new color  when they are co-expressed so the teal  and the purple  co-expressed on a cell give this rich  dark blue which you're seeing  here all throughout the tissue  and there are very few cells which are  strictly purple  or strictly teal get that rich blue  color  when purple and yellow are close for us  we give an orange  which we have one or two cells here not  that many  in this particular project we were able  to explain for this sample  that the antibody one and the antibody  to coexpress  all antibodies three that yell beyond  different  cells and if yellow and two  were together it would give you this  green cell  keeping that co-expression in mind and  adding more colors here's the example of  looking at a tumor micro environment  where we have these cell types that  we're  looking at we're not actually interested  in the collagen  we want to be able to see each color to  60.  so this cd4 is in brown which will not  do a coexpression color with the cd8 in  purple  c and cd19 are different different cell  plates so they're not going to be  co-expressed  and the blue is a monocyte which i've  highlighted here because they are a  little  bit difficult to find if you're not  looking for them  they're all being separate colors so  sometimes when you're multiplexing  with this amp hq you need that whole  expression  like in the previous slide and sometimes  you need them distinctly separate  like in this slide and you have to pick  your chromogens accordingly  looking at slides one by one is a very  helpful way to get  quick context about that pure slide but  we get a lot of projects  through here that need bigger picture  answers than just a one by one  by slide type of description and for  that we move on to tmas  we had a project recently  where it was a low express antigen  on a panel of tumor heights and  this client interested in knowing what  the key patterns are  for their particular subtype that had  high expression versus  low expression and we are able to do  that  by using an amphitheater to detect that  low signal antigen  and then use automated whole slide  imaging  and image analysis to create a  tma map screening for indications  and evaluating the incidence of diseases  in those diseases of interest for the  client  another piece of our state-of-the-art  capabilities  is high quality  100x oil whole side imaging giving us  up nice and close cellular views  of the tissue we are  sometimes using our amp hq kit  technology when staining these samples  and at 100x oil you're still getting  nice  crisp stain it doesn't become fuzzy or  indistinct we still get that nice crisp  staining  on top of that a nice 100x oil allows  for  these stacking images where you get a  better three-dimensional feel  of how the cells are in space  on that tissue as you can see here  on the left we have that nice crisp  cellular look  and on the right still a very pretty 100  x  image but without that added context  that is the stacking allows  so in summary i presented on the  application of  the discovery amp hq technology  at novovita novovita histopath  laboratory is a cro located  in boston massachusetts specializing in  automated histopathology services  including high quality  immunohistochemistry the discovery  amp hq kit provides sensitivity of ihc  assay compared to the standard ihc  assays  which helps to reduce background  and improve signal the  discovery amp hq kit protocols can be  used for a variety of project types  including but not limited the ones i've  described here such as looking at tumor  antigens  reducing backgrounds on old poorly  preserved samples  produced in tmas in both single stained  and  multiplexed assays  before i wrap up i would give a huge  shout out of thanks to the nova  histopath team telling andrew chris  julian  selena and justin were a big help of  putting this presentation together  and finally i been sari elitist  i hope you've learned how to use the faq  kit  and it can provide to your ihc staining  thank you very much for your attention  and i hope you enjoyed the roast  discovery  global user symposium
 

